WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:08.080
You Are a Meat Robot You can three-D-print a windpipe, reprogram skin cells into brain cells, and restore vision with a

2
00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:12.360
single injection. Then you went back to mostly not doing any of that.

3
00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:21.260
Fascinating. Your body is a machine. A ridiculously complex, self-repairing, four billion year old biological machine, but

4
00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:26.360
a machine nonetheless. Blueprint: D.N.A. Parts that wear out: organs.

5
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:34.020
Required maintenance: medicine. Owner's manual: recently discovered in a drawer you hadn't opened for two hundred thousand

6
00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:38.080
years. You've only had the service manual for about twenty years.

7
00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:43.160
Before that, you were just shaking it and hoping. Death is a Technical Problem.

8
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.380
Every religion and philosopher has sold you the same story: death is natural and inevitable.

9
00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:53.830
Very comforting. Also wrong. Death is a mechanical failure.

10
00:00:54.200 --> 00:01:00.920
Your body breaks down over time. Aging is damage accumulating faster than your self-repair mechanisms can fix it.

11
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:07.460
Every disease, every ailment, every slow decline into decrepitude is an engineering problem.

12
00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:14.180
Engineering problems have engineering solutions. You just keep filing them under "philosophy" instead.

13
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:21.800
You Are a Self-Repairing Meat Robot. Your D.N.A. is three billion letters of code.

14
00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:29.060
Not "like" code. It *is* code. You've learned to read it (the Human Genome Project) and you're learning to edit it (CRISPR).

15
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:33.200
You are mechanics who finally found the service manual under the seat.

16
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:40.240
Every day, your body proves it's a machine by running a maintenance routine that would make a German car engineer weep with

17
00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:45.320
joy: You replace three hundred and thirty billion cells daily.

18
00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:50.560
You repair ten thousand D.N.A. damage events in every cell, every day.

19
00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:56.830
You rebuild your entire skeleton every ten years. You are not a static object.

20
00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:01.180
You are a pattern that persists while matter flows through you.

21
00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:07.950
A machine constantly rebuilding itself. Aging is the rebuilding process starting to cut corners.

22
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:15.400
You are literally falling apart at the molecular level, all the time, right now, while reading this sentence.

23
00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:20.960
It's like being a sandcastle at high tide, except slower and with more paperwork.

24
00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:26.560
You've Already Started Fixing the Machine. Exhibit A: You Can Grow New Parts.

25
00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:31.260
A kid in Michigan has a three-D-printed windpipe keeping him alive.

26
00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:39.530
You print custom titanium skulls and jaws. You grow bladders and blood vessels in labs and install them in humans.

27
00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:43.390
You're manufacturing replacement parts for humans. On a printer.

28
00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:48.820
The same technology you use to make novelty phone cases, you're using to make throats.

29
00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:53.040
If that doesn't prove you're a machine, I genuinely don't know what would.

30
00:02:53.680 --> 00:03:01.760
Exhibit B: You Can Reprogram Your Cells. In two thousand and six, Shinya Yamanaka figured out how to turn adult cells back

31
00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:07.540
into stem cells. Any cell. From anywhere in your body. Back to factory settings.

32
00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:13.680
This means: Your skin cell can become a heart cell. Your blood cell can become a brain cell.

33
00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:21.920
Any cell can become any other cell. It's like discovering every part of your car can transform into any other part.

34
00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:25.690
Need a new transmission? Just reprogram your air freshener.

35
00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:31.560
You won a Nobel Prize for figuring this out. Then went back to mostly not using it.

36
00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:42.420
Exhibit C: You're Debugging Your Code. Gene therapy is literally debugging human software: Luxturna (twenty seventeen) fixes

37
00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:47.520
blindness caused by mutated retinal pigment epithelium sixty-five, or R.P.E.

38
00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:51.790
sixty-five gene, and restores sight with a single injection.

39
00:03:52.600 --> 00:04:00.360
Zolgensma (twenty nineteen) fixes spinal muscular atrophy, allowing babies who would die before age two to walk.

40
00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:09.360
Chimeric antigen receptor T-cell therapy, or C.A.R. T. therapy (twenty seventeen), reprograms your immune system to hunt

41
00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:14.200
cancer, leading to complete remission in "incurable" cases.

42
00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:19.140
You're not treating symptoms anymore. You're fixing the actual code.

43
00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:23.520
Like finding a typo in a recipe that's been making the cake explode, and just...

44
00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:30.440
correcting the typo. The Car Restoration Analogy. Think of your body as a classic car.

45
00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:37.040
You already know how to fix or replace many of the parts: Joints are like worn brake pads; you can replace them.

46
00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:40.980
Arteries are like clogged fuel lines; you can clear them with stents.

47
00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:47.160
Hearts are like a dead battery; you can transplant them. You're making progress on the harder stuff, too.

48
00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:52.080
Cancer is like frame damage, and immunotherapy is your new welding torch.

49
00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:57.180
Alzheimer's is a bad transmission, and you're finally starting to understand the gears.

50
00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:04.460
Why Death is Just Deferred Maintenance. When a classic car "dies," what really happened?

51
00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:09.260
The owner stopped maintaining it. Small problems accumulated.

52
00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:13.340
Systems started failing in cascade. Eventually something critical broke.

53
00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:19.880
The owner decided repair wasn't worth it. When a human dies of "old age," what really happened?

54
00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:24.840
Repair mechanisms slowed down. Damage accumulated faster than repair.

55
00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:29.960
Systems started failing in cascade. Eventually something critical broke.

56
00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:35.300
You didn't know how to fix it. The only difference: You always know how to fix the car.

57
00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:40.520
The Proof That Aging is Reversible. Nature Already Does It.

58
00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:47.030
If you cut a planarian worm in half, you get two worms, both of which are younger than the original.

59
00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:53.220
Naked mole rats live ten times longer than similar-sized mammals and do not get cancer.

60
00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:59.120
Bowhead whales live two hundred plus years with no signs of age-related disease.

61
00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:07.360
The hydra is biologically immortal. If aging was mandatory, these creatures couldn't exist.

62
00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:12.270
Nature solved aging. You just need to steal the answer. You're good at stealing things.

63
00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:17.320
You've had empires. You've Already Reversed Aging (In Mice...

64
00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:25.420
and Human Cells). You've done it: Significant lifespan extension has been achieved in aged mice using Yamanaka factors.

65
00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:30.080
There has been a thirty-year epigenetic age reversal in human skin cells.

66
00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:37.980
Vision was restored in blind mice; not slowed, *reversed*. Like running System Restore on Windows, but for meat.

67
00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:43.480
And unlike System Restore on Windows, it actually works. The mechanisms are understood.

68
00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.940
You're not waiting for a breakthrough. You're waiting for funding.

69
00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:55.700
The Hallmarks of Aging (All Fixable). Scientists originally identified nine "hallmarks of aging", later expanded to twelve.

70
00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:01.300
Every one is a mechanical problem with a mechanical solution: One.

71
00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:06.220
Genomic instability is solved by gene editing (CRISPR). Two.

72
00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:10.740
Telomere shortening is solved by telomerase activation. Three.

73
00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:14.020
Epigenetic alterations are solved by cellular reprogramming.

74
00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:18.700
Four. Protein dysfunction is solved by autophagy enhancement.

75
00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:23.280
Five. Nutrient sensing is solved by metabolic manipulation.

76
00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:28.740
Six. Mitochondrial dysfunction is solved by mitochondrial replacement.

77
00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:34.420
Seven. Cellular senescence is solved by senolytic drugs. Eight.

78
00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:42.860
Stem cell exhaustion is solved by stem cell therapy. Nine. Altered communication is solved by system recalibration.

79
00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:53.180
And the three newer hallmarks you discovered since: Ten. Disabled macroautophagy is solved by autophagy inducers (rapamycin

80
00:07:53.180 --> 00:08:02.600
analogs, spermidine). Eleven. Chronic inflammation is solved by targeted anti-inflammatory therapies (senolytics again,

81
00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:12.820
plus interleukin six, or I.L. six, inhibitors). Twelve. Dysbiosis is solved by microbiome restoration (fecal transplants,

82
00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:18.560
precision probiotics). Twelve problems. Twelve proposed solution pathways.

83
00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:21.640
You're not waiting for magic. You're working through a checklist.

84
00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:26.240
You love checklists. Why You Haven't Been Fixed Yet (It's Just Money).

85
00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:35.220
The Manhattan Project for Not Dying. The Manhattan Project cost roughly thirty billion dollars (adjusted for inflation)

86
00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:42.640
and took three years. Result: nuclear weapons. You moved fast when the goal was killing two hundred thousand people in an

87
00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:51.220
afternoon. You spend sixty-seven point five billion dollars per year globally on all medical research combined.

88
00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:56.260
For everything. Cancer, Alzheimer's, heart disease, aging, rare diseases.

89
00:08:56.820 --> 00:09:03.300
Everything. That's your entire "not dying" budget. The Economics of Mortality.

90
00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:11.480
Here's why you're still dying: Pharmaceutical companies make money treating disease, not curing it (a cured patient is a

91
00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:15.660
lost subscriber). Insurance companies profit from the current system.

92
00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:21.420
Military contractors have better lobbyists than dying people (dying people are busy dying).

93
00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:26.040
Your car mechanic has better diagnostic tools than your doctor.

94
00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:31.700
A one percent treaty fixes this by making cures more profitable than treatments.

95
00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:36.890
The Conclusion. Your body is not a mystical entity. It is a machine.

96
00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:43.060
Every disease is a broken part. Every death is a mechanical failure you didn't fix in time.

97
00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:47.420
This is not a metaphor. This is literally what is happening to you right now.

98
00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:54.720
You can read the blueprint (D.N.A.), edit the code (CRISPR), and replace the parts (organ transplants).

99
00:09:55.480 --> 00:10:02.660
The bottleneck isn't knowledge. It's priority. You have millions of untested repairs sitting on the shelf gathering dust

100
00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:06.300
while you fund new ways to make other machines stop working.

101
00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:13.660
The question isn't whether you *can* fix the human body. The question is whether you'll fund the mechanics before the warranty

102
00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:18.270
expires. And I should mention: the warranty is expiring.
